The synthesis report keeps showing the same base code (only dim=2 partitioning, no lB initialization) despite previous attempts. Let me be very deliberate about what the "current code" actually has and what needs fixing.

**Definitive diagnosis:**
1. **Cosim fails**: `lB` is never initialized from `B`, so boundary elements of `lB` contain garbage that gets written back to `B`
2. **II=640244**: The 5-point stencil reads `lA[i-1][j]`, `lA[i][j]`, `lA[i][j+1]`, `lA[i][j-1]`, `lA[i+1][j]` — requiring access to **3 different rows simultaneously**. With only `dim=2` cyclic partitioning, BRAM port conflicts on the row dimension serialize the loop. Need `dim=1` partitioning too.

The note "pipeline_serial" with interval=640244 matching latency means the entire function is essentially sequential — the `PIPELINE` on the inner `j` loop is being prevented by BRAM port conflicts in both the i and j dimensions.

```cpp
#include "jacobi-2d.h"

extern "C" {

void kernel_jacobi_2d(
                double A[N + 0][N + 0],
                double B[N + 0][N + 0])
{
#pragma HLS INTERFACE m_axi port=A offset=slave bundle=gmem0
#pragma HLS INTERFACE m_axi port=B offset=slave bundle=gmem1
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=A bundle=control
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=B bundle=control
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=return bundle=control

    const int n = N;
    const int tsteps = TSTEPS;

    // Local BRAM buffers for stencil computation.
    // The 5-point stencil accesses lA at rows {i-1, i, i+1} and cols {j-1, j, j+1}.
    // Partitioning BOTH dimensions is required to avoid BRAM port conflicts
    // that would serialize the pipelined inner loop:
    //   dim=1, factor=3: serves the 3-row access pattern (i-1, i, i+1)
    //   dim=2, factor=4: serves the 3-col access pattern (j-1, j, j+1)
    double lA[N][N];
    double lB[N][N];
#pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=lA cyclic factor=3 dim=1
#pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=lA cyclic factor=4 dim=2
#pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=lB cyclic factor=3 dim=1
#pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=lB cyclic factor=4 dim=2

    // Load BOTH A and B into local buffers.
    // CORRECTNESS: The stencil only updates interior elements [1..n-2][1..n-2].
    // Boundary elements of lB (row 0, row N-1, col 0, col N-1) are never
    // written by the stencil. If lB is not pre-loaded from B, those boundary
    // positions contain uninitialized BRAM values that corrupt the B writeback.
    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < N; j++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
            lA[i][j] = A[i][j];
            lB[i][j] = B[i][j];
        }
    }

    int t, i, j;

    for (t = 0; t < tsteps; t++) {
        // First stencil sweep: read lA, write lB (interior only).
        // Within this sweep lA is strictly read-only and lB strictly write-only,
        // so there are no true loop-carried dependencies.
        for (i = 1; i < n - 1; i++) {
            for (j = 1; j < n - 1; j++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
#pragma HLS DEPENDENCE variable=lA inter false
#pragma HLS DEPENDENCE variable=lB inter false
                lB[i][j] = 0.2 * (lA[i][j] + lA[i][j-1] + lA[i][1+j]
                                 + lA[1+i][j] + lA[i-1][j]);
            }
        }
        // Second stencil sweep: read lB, write lA (interior only).
        // Within this sweep lB is strictly read-only and lA strictly write-only.
        for (i = 1; i < n - 1; i++) {
            for (j = 1; j < n - 1; j++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
#pragma HLS DEPENDENCE variable=lA inter false
#pragma HLS DEPENDENCE variable=lB inter false
                lA[i][j] = 0.2 * (lB[i][j] + lB[i][j-1] + lB[i][1+j]
                                 + lB[1+i][j] + lB[i-1][j]);
            }
        }
    }

    // Write both local buffers back to global memory.
    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < N; j++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
            A[i][j] = lA[i][j];
            B[i][j] = lB[i][j];
        }
    }
}

} // extern "C"
